// Package screen is the gogo text screen API: the "screen" port. It is a
// stub package like gogo/imu: the compiler type-checks against it and the
// bodies (there are none) are never run. The C side is runtime/screen/ and
// the board's display hook. Most programs use gogo/tui, which builds on it.
//
// The screen is a grid of character cells. Each cell holds one character, a
// foreground and a background colour (0-15, the Color constants) and the
// attributes Bold, Underline and Reverse. A wide (CJK) character takes two
// cells.
//
// Open it with port.Open("screen", screen.Config{}). One actor owns the
// screen at a time (a second Open fails); while it is open the board's text
// console (println) is hidden, and closing the port (or the owner exiting)
// shows the console again. The port first sends its owner one port.Chunk
// describing the grid (Data: cols, rows, colours, attributes, one byte each,
// the attribute byte a mask of Bold | Underline | Reverse). Draw with
// port.Out{Data} commands: Data is a sequence of draw operations (the Op
// constants below), applied together as one frame, and every Out is answered
// with a port.Done (Seq -1: the Out was refused, a malformed operation
// anywhere in it, and nothing of it was drawn). Every Out gets its Done,
// in order, but not always at once: an owner whose memory is nearly full
// gets it when it has room again (the port retries every 10 ms), and with
// 8 Dones waiting a further Out is refused. So wait for a Done with a
// timeout (actor.ReceiveTimeout). A Done's From is the screen port's PID,
// which tells it apart from other ports' Dones.
//
// Operations (each an Op byte followed by its argument bytes; coordinates are
// cells, 0-based, the top-left cell 0, 0):
//
//	OpClear                    blank the clip rectangle in the current colours; cursor to its top-left
//	OpMove x y                 move the cursor (any position; drawing outside the clip is dropped)
//	OpText n bytes[n]          n (1-255) bytes of UTF-8 text at the cursor, which advances; no wrapping
//	OpColor c                  colours: foreground c>>4, background c&15
//	OpAttr a                   attributes: a mask of Bold, Underline, Reverse (other bits: malformed)
//	OpFill x y w h lo hi       fill a rectangle with the character lo | hi<<8 (U+0020 and up, no surrogates)
//	OpClip x y w h             the clip rectangle, cut to the screen; every operation draws only inside it
//	OpScroll x y w h n         scroll a rectangle (cut to the clip) up n rows, n a signed byte (negative: down)
//	OpCursor on                show (1) the cursor at the cursor position (it stays there until the next OpCursor), or hide (0) it
//
// The cells of a wide character stay together: drawing over either half
// blanks the other half, even when that half is outside the clip.
//
// A frame starts with the colours, attributes, clip and cursor position the
// previous one left. The grid starts blank, colours White on Black, no
// attributes, the clip the whole screen, the cursor hidden at 0, 0.
//
// The struct type Config is reserved type 44 (TYPE_SCREEN_CONFIG in
// runtime/vm/opcodes.h), added in image format 15.1: an image that uses it
// is stamped 15.1 and needs a VM of 15.1 or later. Keep its field order in
// sync with compiler.reservedTypes; fields are only ever appended.
package screen

// Config configures the port. Cols and Rows ask for a grid of that size; 0
// means the whole display (40 x 11 on both boards and, by default, on the
// host). A board refuses a grid larger than its display; the host takes up
// to 255 columns and 32 rows.
type Config struct{ Cols, Rows int }

// Operations (see the package comment).
const (
	OpClear  = 1
	OpMove   = 2
	OpText   = 3
	OpColor  = 4
	OpAttr   = 5
	OpFill   = 6
	OpClip   = 7
	OpScroll = 8
	OpCursor = 9
)

// Attributes (OpAttr).
const (
	Bold      = 1
	Underline = 2
	Reverse   = 4
)

// Colours: the 16-colour palette (RGB565 on a board's LCD, the ANSI colours
// on a terminal).
const (
	Black         = 0
	Red           = 1
	Green         = 2
	Yellow        = 3
	Blue          = 4
	Magenta       = 5
	Cyan          = 6
	White         = 7
	Gray          = 8
	BrightRed     = 9
	BrightGreen   = 10
	BrightYellow  = 11
	BrightBlue    = 12
	BrightMagenta = 13
	BrightCyan    = 14
	BrightWhite   = 15
)
